 'context': [Document(id='48d3eb01-a8bc-45ad-ab89-acdd0ff0bbee', metadata={'creationdate': '2020-01-25T10:01:47+01:00', 'creator': 'Edited with https://pdfresizer.com', 'moddate': '2020-01-25T10:01:47+01:00', 'page': 578, 'page_label': '579', 'producer': 'GPL Ghostscript 9.26', 'source': '../rguktBasarDataset/acadamic/Academic_Regulations_Hand_Book.pdf', 'total_pages': 1021}, page_content='Practice: Introduction to Phonetics ñ Speech Sounds ñ Vowels and Consonants -Ice-Breaking \nActivity and JAM Session- Situational Dialogues ñ Greetings ñ Taking Leave ñ Introducing \nOneself and Others. \n \nUNIT ñ II \n \nUnderstand: Structure of Syllables ñ Word Stress and Rhythm ñ Weak Forms and Strong \nForms in Context- Features of Good Conversation ñ Non-verbal Communication. \nPractice: Basic Rules of Word Accent - Stress Shift - W eak Forms and Strong Forms in \nContext-Situational Dialogues ñ Role-Play- Expressions in Various Situations ñMaking \nRequests and Seeking Permissions - Telephone Etiquette. \nUNIT - III \n \nUnderstand: Intonation-Errors in Pronunciation-the Influence of Mother Tongue (MTI)- How to \nmake Formal Presentations. \nPractice: Common Indian Variants in Pronunciation ñ Differences in British and American \nPronunciation- Formal Presentations. \nUNIT ñ IV \nUnderstand: Listening for General Details-Public Speaking ñ Exposure to Structured Talks. \nPractice: Listening Comprehension Tests- Making a Short Spe ech ñ Extempore \nUNIT ñ V \nUnderstand: Listening for Specific Details- Interview Skills. \nPractice:  Listening Comprehension Tests- Mock Interviews. \n \n \nLearning Outcomes: Students will be able to attain \n7. Better understanding of nuances of English language through audi o- visual experience \nand group activities  \n5. Neutralization of accent for intelligibility \n6. Speaking skills with clarity and confidence which in turn enhances their employability skills \n \nSuggested References: \n3. Clarity English Success - Software \n4. Connected Speech- Software \n5. Issues in English 2- Software \n6. \nhttp://www.clarityenglish.com/program/practicalwriting/ \n7. http://www.clarityenglish.com/program/roadtoielts/'),
